# ⚠ WHAT UNION DOES *NOT* DO (kpr-watch-07, measured 2026-08-17)
#
# GitHub does not apply these rules. `.gitattributes` merge drivers are honoured
# by `git merge`, `git rebase` and `git merge-tree` — every path we rehearse on —
# and NOT by the merge GitHub computes for a pull request. So a branch that
# collides on a union path merges clean locally and is reported CONFLICTING /
# DIRTY on the PR, and the two disagree while both are right about their own
# merge. The "plumbing a forge runs server-side" claimed by the rehearsal notes
# below was never GitHub's plumbing; those notes are true about git and wrong
# about the forge.
#
# It is the COMMON case, not an edge: CLAUDE.md requires every PR that adds a
# test file to append to `args/ci_test_files/core.txt`, so nearly every kanban PR
# collides there. Nine of ten open PRs were DIRTY that morning; re-running each
# merge with the union rules stripped reproduced the forge's verdict on ten of
# ten, negative control included (#1730 appended to `core.txt`, did not collide
# there, and was the one PR reported MERGEABLE).
#
# Union still earns its place — it removes the collision from every local merge
# and rebase, which is what makes the recovery below deterministic. What it does
# not do is stop the PR going DIRTY. THE FIX IS A REBASE: it applies the union
# rule and writes the resolution into the branch, after which the forge has
# nothing left to object to. `pr_watcher.classify_conflict` names that case
# `union_only` and rebases it, and `_rebase_attempts` counts attempts per base
# era so a collision that returns on every push to main cannot exhaust a budget
# sized for a one-off. Do NOT respond to a DIRTY union PR by removing a rule
# here — that puts the conflict back in every local merge as well.
#
# Append-only coordination files (kax-conflict-03)
#
# The tool manifest concentrates edits from unrelated tasks into one markdown
# table per topic: 219 commits in the 14 days to 2026-08-08, 95 added rows
# against 22 removed on the three hottest shards alone. Two branches appending
# a row to the same table conflict every time, and the resolution is always
# "take the superset of both rows" — which is exactly what merge=union does.
#
# `tools/ci/pr_watcher.py::_ADDITIVE_PATH_MARKERS` already classified these
# paths as "union-merged coordination files"; until now nothing implemented it.
#
# Verified conflict-free for 2/3/5 concurrent branches in BOTH the local
# `git merge` path and the bare `git merge-tree --write-tree` plumbing a forge
# runs server-side:  python tools/git/manifest_merge_rehearsal.py
#
# Scope note: union is safe here because these files are flat, line-oriented
# tables whose rows are independent. Do NOT extend it to YAML/JSON/Python —
# a union merge there produces duplicate keys or syntactically broken code.
# Its one cost is that a row DELETED on one branch is resurrected when that
# hunk conflicts with an append on another; `coherence_checker`'s manifest
# check reports the byte-identical duplicate rows this leaves behind.
tools/manifest.md merge=union
tools/manifest/*.md merge=union
icdev/tools/manifest.md merge=union
icdev/tools/manifest/*.md merge=union

# CI test allowlists (kax-conflict-07)
#
# These lines were extracted from a shell line-continuation chain inside
# .github/workflows/icdev-ci.yml, where every task that added a test file
# appended at the same offset in the same hand-written file. Five open PRs
# collided on it in one night (2026-08-09), and because
# `pr_watcher.hold_on_sibling_conflict` refuses to co-merge PRs sharing a
# non-additive file, each of the five became a sibling of every other and the
# board deadlocked. #1434's derived-file exclusion did not cover it: the
# workflow is hand-written, and two PRs editing a job's `run:` block IS a
# collision worth serializing. Only the LIST is additive — so only the list moved.
#
# Same safety argument as the manifest shards above: flat, line-oriented, one
# independent pytest target per line, so the superset of two appends is the
# correct resolution. `tools/ci/gated_test_list.py --check` catches the two things
# union can leave behind (a duplicated entry, a resurrected deletion) plus the
# property the explicit list existed for in the first place — it fails RED when
# the list is empty, below its floor, or names a path that is not in the tree.
#
# Verified conflict-free for 2/3/5 concurrent branches in BOTH the local
# `git merge` path and the bare `git merge-tree --write-tree` plumbing a forge
# runs server-side:  python tools/git/ci_test_list_merge_rehearsal.py
args/ci_test_files/*.txt merge=union
icdev/data/args/ci_test_files/*.txt merge=union

# CI skip census (trust-disc-03)
#
# Same shape and same argument as the allowlists above: one independent site per
# line, `<file>::<qualname>::<kind>[<n>]  # <written reason>`, appended to by
# every task that registers or removes a skip. Two branches touching different
# skips would otherwise collide at the same end-of-file offset, and the
# resolution is always "keep both lines".
#
# `tools/ci/skip_census.py --check` catches what union can leave behind: it fails
# on a duplicated site (tested) and on a malformed line, and a resurrected
# deletion shows up as a stale entry the next --prune clears. The ceiling
# `skip_census.skip_max` fails independently if the census grew.
#
# There is deliberately NO `icdev/data/args/` mirror line here. The allowlist
# block above has one because that file really is mirrored into the package;
# this one is not — `args/ci_skip_census.txt` is the only copy in the tree, and
# `skip_census.CENSUS_FILE` is the only thing that reads it. The mirror line was
# copied from the block above when trust-disc-03 landed and matched nothing for
# as long as it existed, which is why kax-conflict-11 added a test that every
# union pattern resolves to a real path.
args/ci_skip_census.txt merge=union

# Command reference (kax-conflict-11)
#
# The largest collision surface still unprotected: `docs/reference/commands.md`
# was touched by 18 of the last 40 branches, against 27 for the CI allowlists
# and 6 for the manifest shards — both of which are already union and neither of
# which conflicted. Two of the six conflicts resolved BY HAND on 2026-08-16 were
# this file, and both resolutions were "keep both blocks", which is what union
# does. The registration checklist in CLAUDE.md tells every task that adds a
# tool to document it here, so the collisions are mandated by process, not
# incidental.
#
# It is also the SAME defect kax-conflict-03 fixed for the manifest shards, one
# file later, and the reason the collisions surfaced as conflicts rather than as
# a deadlock: `tools/git/coordination_paths.py::COORDINATION_PATH_MARKERS`
# already lists `docs/reference/commands.md`, so `pr_watcher`'s
# `hold_on_sibling_conflict` has been treating two PRs touching it as normal and
# declining to serialize them — a promise that they merge without a human, which
# nothing implemented on the git side. The classification and the merge driver
# disagreed, and the branches paid for it.
#
# Unlike a manifest shard this file is BLOCK-structured — a fenced ```bash
# region holding a comment paragraph and several command lines — so "obviously
# append-only" was an assumption worth measuring rather than asserting. Over the
# 60 most recent merges on origin/main, of the branches whose OWN diff against
# the merge base touched this file:
#
#     pure additions (union merges correctly) ..... 14
#     also removed or edited lines ................ 0
#
# Every branch appends; nothing edits an existing line. Verified conflict-free
# for 2/3/5 concurrent branches with code fences intact, in BOTH the local
# `git merge` path and the bare `git merge-tree --write-tree` plumbing a forge
# runs server-side:
#     python tools/git/commands_doc_merge_rehearsal.py --branches 5
# and the harness proves it is measuring a real collision rather than passing
# vacuously:
#     python tools/git/commands_doc_merge_rehearsal.py --without-union
#
# Same scope note as above — flat, line-oriented, independent lines. Union's one
# cost here is that two branches appending the SAME command line leave a
# byte-identical duplicate, and that a line deleted on one branch is resurrected
# when its hunk collides with an append. `coherence_checker`'s
# `doc_command_paths` check catches the dangerous half of that directly: a
# resurrected command whose file no longer exists FAILS the gate. A duplicate is
# cosmetic and visible in the diff — and note 31 duplicate command lines already
# exist here legitimately (the same command documented in two sections), so a
# blanket no-duplicates rule would fire on real content and is deliberately NOT
# imposed.
docs/reference/commands.md merge=union
icdev/docs/reference/commands.md merge=union
icdev/data/docs/reference/commands.md merge=union

